-- title:
--   Theorem 3.2 — every policy is a $\bar\lambda$-mixture of nonrandomized policies
-- statement:
--   Let $\bar\lambda$ be the countable product of Lebesgue measures on $[0,1]$. In the Borel-space MDP with analytic constraint graph $\Gamma$, for each policy $\pi\in\Pi$ there exists a family of nonrandomized policies $\bar f(\bar\theta)\in\Pi'$, parametrized by $\bar\theta\in[0,1]^\infty$, such that
--   1. the mapping $(p_0,\bar\theta)\mapsto\rho_{p_0}[\bar f(\bar\theta)]$ from $\mathcal P(\mathbb X)\times[0,1]^\infty$ into $\mathcal P(\Omega)$ is universally measurable;
--   2. for all $p_0\in\mathcal P(\mathbb X)$,
--   $$\mathbb P^\pi_{p_0}(E)=\int_{[0,1]^\infty}\mathbb P^{\bar f(\bar\theta)}_{p_0}(E)\,\bar\lambda(d\bar\theta)\qquad\forall\,E\in\mathcal U(\Omega). \tag{3.1}$$
--
--   Moreover, if $\pi$ is Markov (semi-Markov), the policies $\bar f(\bar\theta)$ are Markov (semi-Markov) as well.
--
--   Randomized policies therefore add nothing beyond mixtures of nonrandomized ones; this is what reduces optimization over $\tilde{\mathcal S}_\star$ to optimization over $\tilde{\mathcal S}'_\star$ in Theorem 3.3(iii).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: universal measurability of $(p_0,\bar\theta)\mapsto\rho_{p_0}[\bar f(\bar\theta)]$ w.r.t. the Borel $\sigma$-algebras of the weak topologies → w.r.t. Mathlib's $\sigma$-algebras on `ProbabilityMeasure` generated by $p\mapsto p(B)$; reason: they coincide for Borel spaces (Bertsekas–Shreve Prop. 7.25)
--   - encoding: the mapping $(p_0,\bar\theta)\mapsto\rho_{p_0}[\bar f(\bar\theta)]$ → an explicit witness $\Psi$ whose values are strategic measures of $\bar f(\bar\theta)$; reason: strategic measures are unique, so $\Psi$ is that mapping
--   - encoding: $\mathbb P^\pi_{p_0}(E)$ on $\mathcal U(\Omega)$ → the value of $\rho_{p_0}[\pi]$ at $E$ (Mathlib outer measure); reason: for universally measurable $E$ the outer measure equals the completion's value
--   - encoding: Markov and semi-Markov policies as kernels depending only on $x_n$, resp. $(x_0,x_n)$ → kernels factoring through universally measurable kernels on $\mathbb A$ given $\mathbb X$, resp. $\mathbb X^2$; reason: the two descriptions give the same policies
--   - encoding: analytic sets as the empty set or images of Borel subsets of Polish spaces under Borel maps (p. 5) → Mathlib's `AnalyticSet` (empty or a continuous image of the Baire space); reason: the two agree for subsets of a Borel space (Bertsekas–Shreve Prop. 7.41)
--   - generalization: the model includes a lower semianalytic one-stage cost $c$ → the model has no cost; reason: the statement does not involve $c$
-- source:
--   Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, Theorem 3.2, p. 8, eq. (3.1); λ̄ defined just before it

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BertsekasShreve_AnalyticSelection_BorelSpace
import Definitions.Def_YuStrategic_Optimality_Mixture

namespace YuStrategic.Optimality

open MeasureTheory BertsekasShreve.AnalyticSelection

/-- **Theorem 3.2** (Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, p. 8). Let `λ̄` be the countable product of Lebesgue
measures on `[0, 1]`. For each `π ∈ Π` there exists a family of nonrandomized policies
`f̄(θ̄) ∈ Π′`, parametrized by `θ̄ ∈ [0, 1]^∞`, such that
1. the mapping `(p₀, θ̄) ↦ ρ_{p₀}[f̄(θ̄)]` from `P(X) × [0, 1]^∞` into `P(Ω)` is universally
   measurable;
2. for all `p₀ ∈ P(X)`, `P^π_{p₀}(E) = ∫_{[0,1]^∞} P^{f̄(θ̄)}_{p₀}(E) λ̄(dθ̄)` for all `E ∈ U(Ω)` (3.1).

Moreover, if `π` is Markov (semi-Markov), the policies `f̄(θ̄)` are Markov (semi-Markov) as well.

**Formalization Note.** All clauses are packed in `IsMixtureFamily M π f̄`; see its docstring for
the σ-algebras and for the evaluation of measures at universally measurable sets. -/
theorem theorem_3_2 {X A : Type} [TopologicalSpace X] [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]
    [IsBorelSpace X] [TopologicalSpace A] [MeasurableSpace A] [BorelSpace A] [IsBorelSpace A]
    (M : Model X A) (π : Policy M) :
    ∃ fbar : (ℕ → unitInterval) → Policy M, IsMixtureFamily M π fbar := by sorry

end YuStrategic.Optimality
